abstract: >-
  GAPS (Guidance for Artifact Preparation and Sharing) is an 
  Open Educational Resource designed to support researchers in 
  preparing, documenting, validating, publishing, preserving, and 
  reusing research artifacts, particularly in Empirical Software 
  Engineering research.

  GAPS provides practical guidance to help researchers identify 
  and address relevant aspects of artifact preparation and sharing 
  throughout the research lifecycle. It is intended to support 
  informed decisions and progressive improvement, rather than 
  prescribe a single workflow or establish a universal standard 
  for artifact quality.

  The guidance is not intended to be exhaustive, and not all 
  recommendations are essential or applicable to every research 
  artifact. Their relevance depends on the artifact's purpose, 
  characteristics, and intended use, as well as the level of 
  coverage and transparency researchers aim to provide. Practical 
  considerations, such as available time, funding, personnel, 
  technical resources, and ethical or legal restrictions, may 
  also influence which recommendations can be implemented.

  Researchers are therefore encouraged to use GAPS selectively, 
  identifying and prioritizing recommendations that are relevant to 
  their context and feasible to address. Some recommendations may be 
  essential for a particular artifact, while others may be optional, 
  unnecessary, or disproportionate to its purpose. Addressing relevant 
  recommendations incrementally can still contribute to improving an 
  artifact, even when it is not feasible or appropriate to implement 
  every recommendation.

  GAPS is a reference to support researchers' judgment, not a 
  compliance checklist that every artifact must satisfy in full. 
  Its purpose is to help researchers make artifact preparation more 
  deliberate and transparent, while recognizing that appropriate 
  practices vary across research contexts.
